text:
sam_customer: Hey team — running into intermittent truncated final output when using the JS SDK in the browser (EventSource + AbortController). After a token refresh/reconnect the last chunk sometimes ends mid-word and the stream immediately closes. Happened on production traffic intermittently. trace_id=tr_0a9f3b4c. Repro steps below.

sam_customer: Repro (minimal):
```js
const es = new EventSource(url, {headers: {Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`}});
const controller = new AbortController();
// start streaming, then simulate token refresh mid-stream
setTimeout(() => {
  controller.abort();
  // then immediately re-open EventSource with new token
  const es2 = new EventSource(url, {headers: {Authorization: `Bearer ${newToken}`}});
}, 1200);
```

sam_customer: Observed output: response text completes most of the time, but ~10% requests end with something like `"...and the final respo"` (truncated). No obvious 401 in network tab.

support-bot: Ticket #INC-4721 created and linked to trace_id tr_0a9f3b4c.

maya_support: Thanks for the detailed repro, Sam. Quick Qs: which SDK version, which browser/version, and is this using our browser helper or raw EventSource? Any console/network logs (HAR) you can paste?

sam_customer: Using @redwood/sdk v2.3.1, Chrome 121 (mac). We use the built-in EventSource helper from the SDK (sdk.stream()). Network shows 200 then stream close, no 401s. Attaching a tiny excerpt from network console: 
```
200 GET /v1/generate  -> streaming frames
frame: {"event":"message","data":"...and the final respo"}
frame: [DONE]
connection: closed by server
```

maya_support: Got it. I'm escalating to eng since this looks like a streaming parser/flush issue on reconnect. @jordan_dev can you take a look?

jordan_dev: Investigating. I can repro locally with the minimal script. Looks like a regression introduced in 2.3.1 where heartbeat handling and parser flush race when AbortController.abort() is called immediately before reconnect. Parser can drop the last buffered token if the heartbeat clears the buffer first.

jordan_dev: Quick mitigation: install the canary build we pushed with a flush ordering fix: `npm i @redwood/sdk@2.3.2-canary.1` and try again. Alternatively as a workaround, wait ~50-200ms after abort before re-opening the stream. Code: 
```js
controller.abort();
setTimeout(() => startStream(newToken), 120); // small delay to let parser flush
```

ken_qa: Confirmed repro with an automated test that does abort+reopen within 0-10ms. With 2.3.1 we reproduce truncation ~12/100 runs. With canary it's 0/500. Root cause: heartbeat timer could call `parser.reset()` concurrent with `parser.flush()` on reconnect. Fix reorders shutdown to flush before reset.

sam_customer: Installing the canary now... `npm i @redwood/sdk@2.3.2-canary.1`

sam_customer: ✅ Verified on prod-like load for 30m: no truncated final tokens observed. Workaround delay also fixed it in our tests. Huge thanks. :tada:

jordan_dev: Great news — we'll merge the fix to main and release 2.3.2 today with the parser flush ordering change and a short note in the changelog about streaming robustness.

maya_support: Marking incident resolved for customer, will follow up with release notes and CI regression test. Thanks everyone. :thumbsup:
